<paragraphs>
        <title>Trying Sage Cells in General</title>
        <p>These examples come from the guide</p>

<sage>
<input>numerical_integral(sin(x)^2, (0, 2))</input>
<!-- need to use wildcard matching to make this doctestable -->
</sage>


        <sage doctest="absolute" tolerance="0.006">
 <input>12 + 0.01*random()</input>
 <output>12.005</output>
 </sage>

 <p> I removed the output line from the first example, so it will not be doctested(?). Not sure what that means exactly... </p>
</paragraphs>


<p>The above was in paragraphs tag so I could put in a header/title. The below is not.</p>

<p> Now, we're going to try some R code cells. First examples come from book. Then I try out a t-test. </p>

 <sage language="r">
 <input>
 ruth &lt;- c(22, 25, 34, 35, 41, 41, 46, 46, 46, 47, 49, 54, 54, 59, 60)
 bonds &lt;- c(16, 25, 24, 19, 33, 25, 34, 46, 37, 33, 42, 40, 37, 34, 49, 73, 46, 45, 45, 5, 26, 28)
 dimaggio &lt;- c(12, 14, 20, 21, 25, 29, 30, 30, 31, 32, 32, 39, 46)
 summary(ruth)
 summary(bonds)
 summary(dimaggio)
 boxplot(ruth, bonds, dimaggio)
 </input>
 </sage>

 <p> The above is a boxplot of the three data sets. Notice that we only get the boxplot output. We do not see the results of the
  three summary() commands. If we want to see this output, we need to add a print() command, or use a different code cell for each. 
</p>
 

 <sage language="r">
 <input>
 # load package and data
 library(ggplot2)
 data(mpg, package="ggplot2")
 mpg_select &lt;- mpg[mpg$manufacturer %in% c("audi", "ford", "honda", "hyundai"), ]
 # Scatterplot
 g &lt;- ggplot(mpg_select, aes(displ, cty)) +
 labs(subtitle="mpg: Displacement vs City Mileage",
 title="Bubble chart")
 g + geom_jitter(aes(col=manufacturer, size=hwy)) +
 geom_smooth(aes(col=manufacturer), method="lm", se=F)
 </input>
 </sage>

 <p> Below we have some empty R cells that you can just type into. I left a comment in one. Notice that the empty cell with
  no input portion does not require a closing tag for sage.
 </p>

 <sage language="r"/>

 <sage language="r">
 <input>
 # All code cells are connected to the same R session, so you can use variables defined in one cell in another cell.
 </input>
 </sage>



<p> Now, let's try a t-test. </p>
  <sage language="r">
 <input>
 t.test(ruth, bonds, paired=F, var.equal=F)
 </input>
 </sage>






 

